    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">{description}</p>

    <p>If you've searched "solar panel grants UK," you've probably seen big claims about free installations. The reality is more nuanced — most homeowners won't get a cash grant, but there's still real financial support worth understanding.</p>

    <h2>The Honest Answer: Most People Won't Get a Free Grant</h2>
    <p>Fully-funded solar installations exist, but they're targeted at specific households — typically those on low incomes, in fuel poverty, or living in homes with poor energy efficiency ratings (EPC D–G). If that's not your situation, you're unlikely to get panels installed for free, but you're not without options.</p>

    <h2>ECO4 Scheme</h2>
    <p>ECO4 requires large energy suppliers to fund efficiency upgrades — sometimes including solar — for qualifying households, usually those on certain benefits or in fuel poverty. It's confirmed to run until December 2026, so there's still time to check eligibility if this applies to you.</p>

    <h2>Warm Homes: Local Grant (England)</h2>
    <p>This is the main scheme for low-income homeowners in England, aimed at privately-owned properties (owned by you or your landlord) with an EPC rating of D to G. It's the successor to older schemes and is rolling out through local councils.</p>

    <h2>Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Scotland:</strong> Home Energy Scotland offers support for households earning £36,000 or less, worth around £6,000 toward installation (part grant, part interest-free loan).</li>
      <li><strong>Wales:</strong> The Nest scheme provides advice and funding support for eligible households.</li>
      <li><strong>Northern Ireland:</strong> There's currently no dedicated solar grant, though the 0% VAT relief below still applies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Almost Everyone Can Get: 0% VAT</h2>
    <p>Regardless of income, solar panels, battery storage, and installation are currently zero-rated for VAT across the UK — worth roughly £1,200–£1,600 in savings on a typical install. This relief is confirmed until March 2027.</p>

    <h2>Smart Export Guarantee (SEG)</h2>
    <p>Not a grant, but ongoing income — SEG pays you for any surplus electricity your panels generate and export back to the grid. Over time, this offsets a meaningful chunk of your installation cost.</p>

    <h2>Solar Together</h2>
    <p>A group-buying scheme run through local councils, where homeowners register interest and installers then bid to win the group's business — often bringing prices down through bulk buying.</p>

    <h2>A Government Loan Scheme Is Coming</h2>
    <p>The government has confirmed it's developing a low or zero-interest loan scheme through the Warm Homes Fund, aimed at homeowners who don't qualify for free grants. Details on rates and eligibility are expected later in 2026 — worth checking back on if you don't currently qualify for anything above.</p>

